{"A":"Research a low-risk exact filter: before permutation search, compare vertex counts, adjacency-matrix row-popcount multisets, and optionally neighborhood signatures. Derive signatures from the parsed matrices, not raw edge counts, and treat row-popcounts consistently with loops. Keep the existing permutation order and output behavior; benchmark whether filter overhead pays on easy cases.","B":"Research exact pruning while preserving the reference's observable traversal and output. Explore color refinement and partial compatibility checks in a traversal equivalent to the source's Heap permutation order; prune only when a necessary isomorphism condition fails. Preserve the identity fast path and matching-permutation output behavior unless validation confirms the harness accepts different output. Prove equivalence and test broadly before proposing integration.","C":"Research a faster exact permutation verifier: compare contiguous byte or packed-bit adjacency representations, precompute reusable row/signature data, and reduce per-candidate lookup overhead without changing which permutations are emitted or their order. Measure the full program, including representation setup, rather than assuming the verifier is the bottleneck.","G":"Integrate only candidates that pass correctness against the reference and improve measured end-to-end time; do not assume gains add. The baseline is valid on 4 cases at mean 0.00841449573636055 s (score 0.5), on ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPU. This is a short runtime, so favor low-overhead changes and do not assume threading helps. Preserve the configured compiler, budgets, tests, oracle, target, and source constraints.","analysis":"The only supplied implementation is the exhaustive graph-isomorphism solver. It checks the identity mapping first and returns immediately if it matches; otherwise it traverses Heap permutations and prints each match it finds. That behavior can differ from the statement's description of a single correspondence, so output compatibility is a key risk. The baseline establishes correctness only for 4 cases and provides no profiling evidence; proposed work should remain exact for all valid inputs and be judged by measured end-to-end results."}